1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:03,767
Assalamualaikum and good day.

2
00:00:03,767 --> 00:00:10,500
My name is Abdul Rahman bin Japry,
matriculation number MET264016.

3
00:00:10,900 --> 00:00:14,133
This video is prepared for METE1153-51,

4
00:00:14,133 --> 00:00:20,500
Occupational Safety for Energy System,
as part of Individual Assignment 4.

5
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:23,267
Today, I will be explaining
the basic concept of

6
00:00:23,267 --> 00:00:26,500
Electrical Hazard and
Operability Study, or E-HAZOP.

7
00:00:27,567 --> 00:00:31,067
Electricity powers almost
every aspect of modern life.

8
00:00:31,067 --> 00:00:33,867
But behind this essential
energy source are complex

9
00:00:33,867 --> 00:00:37,000
electrical systems that can
present serious hazards.

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,133
What happens when an electrical
system does not operate as intended?

11
00:00:41,167 --> 00:00:43,467
And how can we identify these risks before

12
00:00:43,467 --> 00:00:46,167
they lead to accidents or equipment failure?

13
00:00:46,433 --> 00:00:48,667
This is where E-HAZOP comes in.

14
00:00:49,433 --> 00:00:53,167
E-HAZOP stands for Electrical
Hazard and Operability Study.

15
00:00:53,167 --> 00:00:57,833
It is a systematic and structured technique
used to identify potential hazards,

16
00:00:57,833 --> 00:01:01,733
operational problems and deviations
within electrical systems.

17
00:01:01,733 --> 00:01:03,567
It applies the principles of the

18
00:01:03,567 --> 00:01:06,800
traditional HAZOP methodology
to electrical designs.

19
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:09,733
Rather than simply inspecting
individual equipment,

20
00:01:09,733 --> 00:01:12,600
E-HAZOP examines how the
overall electrical system

21
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:15,733
is designed and how different
components interact.

22
00:01:15,733 --> 00:01:18,800
The objective is to identify
possible problems early,

23
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:22,733
assess their consequences and
recommend appropriate safeguards.

24
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:25,567
So, how does E-HAZOP work?

25
00:01:25,567 --> 00:01:29,333
First, the engineering team
reviews important design documents,

26
00:01:29,333 --> 00:01:32,000
such as electrical single-line diagrams.

27
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,833
Next, the electrical system is divided
into smaller sections, known as nodes.

28
00:01:36,833 --> 00:01:40,800
The team then applies guidewords to
electrical parameters such as voltage,

29
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:43,133
current, power, frequency and earthing.

30
00:01:43,167 --> 00:01:46,300
For example, guidewords such
as 'over', 'under', 'spike',

31
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:48,500
'dip' and 'fault' help the team consider

32
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:51,667
possible deviations from
normal operating conditions.

33
00:01:51,667 --> 00:01:55,567
Each deviation is analysed by
identifying its possible causes,

34
00:01:55,567 --> 00:01:58,833
consequences and existing
protective safeguards.

35
00:01:58,833 --> 00:02:01,100
Finally, recommendations and corrective

36
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:03,900
actions are documented in a hazard register.

37
00:02:04,467 --> 00:02:08,233
Consider a practical example
involving a power transformer.

38
00:02:08,567 --> 00:02:12,400
What if the transformer experiences
an overvoltage condition?

39
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:15,367
This deviation could be
caused by incorrect protection

40
00:02:15,367 --> 00:02:18,633
settings or disturbances
within the electrical network.

41
00:02:18,633 --> 00:02:21,333
If not properly controlled,
it may place stress on

42
00:02:21,333 --> 00:02:24,400
the insulation and potentially
damage the equipment.

43
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:28,067
During an E-HAZOP study, the team
examines the possible causes,

44
00:02:28,067 --> 00:02:31,933
consequences and effectiveness
of existing protection systems.

45
00:02:31,933 --> 00:02:33,967
The team can then recommend additional

46
00:02:33,967 --> 00:02:36,833
safeguards or corrective
actions where necessary.

47
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:39,467
E-HAZOP plays an important role in

48
00:02:39,467 --> 00:02:42,867
improving electrical safety
and system reliability.

49
00:02:42,867 --> 00:02:46,833
It helps protect personnel, reduce
the likelihood of equipment damage,

50
00:02:46,833 --> 00:02:50,433
identify design weaknesses and
support business continuity.

51
00:02:50,467 --> 00:02:53,567
It is particularly valuable
during the design stage,

52
00:02:53,567 --> 00:02:58,300
as well as when electrical systems
undergo modifications or major upgrades.

53
00:02:58,300 --> 00:03:02,067
A multidisciplinary team is essential,
bringing together engineering,

54
00:03:02,067 --> 00:03:04,367
operations and maintenance knowledge.

55
00:03:04,933 --> 00:03:07,300
Ultimately, E-HAZOP is about identifying

56
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:10,900
potential problems before
they become serious incidents.

57
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:15,867
Because in electrical safety, prevention
is always better than failure.

58
00:03:15,867 --> 00:03:16,933
Thank you.
